If this is about user consent, there is little sense in having:

one application an authorization from user through another application (which is actually the same application);
the same application prompting the user consent and telling the first application (which is actually the same application) that the consent has been granted.

You could use a OAuth authorization code flow with PKCE from browser context and still have mobile-app-backed strong authentication:

the browser-based application makes an authorization request by redirecting to the authorization server URL (https://as.example.com/auth?scope=...&redirect_uri=...);
the authorization server UI provides your with an option for "authenticated with my mobile app";
the authorization server communicates with your mobile app which may be used to authenticate (including any biometric whatnot necessary) and/or grant consent;
end of the OAuth flow.

Imagine the following (very hypothetical, probably not even valid C) code:
typedef struct
{
   uint8_t buffer[128];
   uint32_t mode;
} thing_t;

process_data(uint8_t *received_buffer, size_t received_len)
{
    thing_t thing;

    thing.mode = 0;
    memcpy(thing.buffer, received_buffer, received_len);

    other_function(thing);
}

If I send data that is longer than the 128-byte limit, I will clobber mode.

